FICTION WRITING: BRUTAL TRUTH WARNING SIGNS & PITFALLS TO AVOID
Objective Warning Signs (Measurable Issues)

Chapter length inconsistency - Wildly varying chapter lengths without narrative justification signal pacing problems

Character name confusion - Multip
Multiple characters with similar names or inconsistent name usage throughout manuscript
Timeline contradictions - Events occurring out of logical sequence or contradicting previously established timeframes

Point-of-view violations - Head-hopping or inconsistent POV that confuses reader understanding

Dialogue tag overuse - Every speech act tagged with "he said/she said" rather than using action beats

Repetitive sentence structure - Every sentence following same grammatical pattern creating monotonous rhythm

Inconsistent world-building details - Physical descriptions or rules changing between chapters without explanation

Unresolved plot threads - Subplots or conflicts introduced but never addressed or concluded

Character motivation gaps - Actions taken without clear reasoning that readers can understand

Setting description overload - Multiple paragraphs of pure description that halt narrative momentum

Subjective Warning Signs (Reader Experience Issues)

Characters all sound identical - No distinct voice differences between different character dialogue

Emotional beats feel manipulative - Dramatic moments that seem designed to force reader response

Stakes never feel genuinely threatening - Conflicts that readers don't believe will have real consequences

Convenient coincidences save the day - Problems solved through unlikely circumstances rather than character action

Romance feels obligatory - Romantic subplots that serve no narrative purpose beyond genre expectations

Villains lack realistic motivation - Antagonists who are evil without believable reasons for their behavior

Dialogue serves only plot function - Conversations that exist solely to convey necessary information

Pacing feels artificially accelerated - Story events happening too quickly without proper development time

Themes become preachy messages - Story stops to deliver obvious moral lessons rather than letting themes emerge naturally

Resolution ties up too neatly - All problems solved perfectly without realistic complications or consequences

Critical System Failures (Emergency Intervention Required)

Multiple power moves deployed simultaneously - Overcomplicating scenes by trying to fix everything at once

Version rescue missions becoming frequent - Sign of fundamental structural problems rather than minor inconsistencies

Reader feedback consistently negative - Beta readers or editors reporting same problems repeatedly across different sections

Author losing emotional connection - Writer no longer feels excited about or invested in their own story

Story premise fundamentally flawed - Core concept cannot support full novel length without major restructuring

EMERGENCY PROTOCOLS

When Multiple Warning Signs Appear

STOP current writing immediately

ASSESS which power moves are most relevant

PRIORITIZE structural issues before stylistic concerns

IMPLEMENT one power move at a time

VERIFY improvements before continuing

DOCUMENT lessons learned for future reference

Version Rescue Escalation

Level 1: Minor inconsistencies (single power move deployment)
Level 2: Chapter conflicts (multiple power move coordination)
Level 3: Major structural issues (full system reset required)
Level 4: Complete rewrite necessary (preserve salvageable elements only)


SYSTEM MAINTENANCE & UPDATES

Regular System Checkpoints

After every 10,000 words written
Before and after major plot developments
When switching between character perspectives
During revision and editing phases
After incorporating feedback from beta readers

Documentation Requirements

Power move deployment log with effectiveness ratings
Version rescue mission records with resolution methods
Warning sign tracking with prevention strategies developed
Reader feedback correlation with system interventions
Continuous improvement notes for future projects


